title: Saved workspaces
tags: GUI
kind: reference
aliases: saved-workspace default-workspace
summary: A workspace saved under a name in its repository — its layout alone, or the work in progress too — loaded back here or in a new workspace, one of them chosen as what every new workspace on the repository opens on.

A workspace can be ''saved'' under a name and ''loaded'' back later, after a restart
included. A saved workspace belongs to its repository: it is kept in the repository's
`.metafolder/`, and only a workspace on that repository can save or load it. What
should hold for every repository is the GUI's configuration instead
(`[panel-defaults]`, [[GUI configuration]]).

!! Two kinds: layout and work

|!Kind |!Holds |!For |
|`layout` |the panel type of each slot that shows the workspace, whether it is shown with one panel or two, and the ''layout variables'' — how the panels show things: the columns of the list and their widths, the field `path-ref` opens on, the zoom of each panel… |preferences: how this repository is looked at |
|`work` |a layout, plus the ''work variables'' — what the panels show: the list's query, the file manager's folder, `path-ref`'s scope, the selection |a line of work, taken up again where it was left |

A `work` is a `layout` and more: loading one restores the layout it was saved with, and
its layout alone can be taken out of it — the file keeps the two apart (below).

!! Which variables are saved

A panel type decides for its own variables ([[Workspace variables]]): a `saved.json`
in its directory lists those that belong to the layout and those that belong to the
work, by name without the panel's prefix. The two lists do not overlap: `work` names
only what is not layout already, since a work save holds both.

```
{
  "layout": ["columns", "column-widths", "mode", "page-size", "finder-fields"],
  "work": ["query", "normal-query", "normal-frozen", "finder", "order-field"]
}
```

The GUI reads every panel type's file at each save, whether the panel is mounted or
not: a panel type not opened this session still keeps its variables in the next save,
and a file edited since the start is taken as it is now. A variable no list names is
never saved, and a panel type without `saved.json` saves nothing. The standard variables are the GUI's: `selected_metarecord`,
`selected_paths`, `selected_metarecords` and `selected_path_ref` are work,
`view.zoom.<panel type>` is layout, and `active_repo`, `metarecords:dirty`,
`pick_request` and `pick_result` are never saved.

What a panel keeps outside the variables — its scroll position, a half-typed draft —
is not saved, nor is what a panel only publishes for others to read
(`metarecord-list:effective-query`, `metarecord-list:sort`).

|!Panel |!Layout |!Work |
|[[metarecord-list|metarecord-list panel]] |`columns`, `column-widths`, `mode`, `page-size`, `finder-fields` |`query`, `normal-query`, `normal-frozen`, `finder`, `order-field` |
|[[path-ref|path-ref panel]] |`field`, `ref-field` |`scope` |
|[[file-manager|file-manager panel]] | |`dir`, `cursor` |
|[[recent|recent panel]] |`sort` | |

`crates/gui/tests/saved_workspaces.rs` checks that every shipped declaration reads, keeps
the two lists apart, and names variables its panel's sources use.

!! The commands

* [[workspace:save]] `<layout|work> <name>` saves the focused workspace under that name. The name is asked with the saved workspaces' names as completions, so what exists already shows while typing; a name that exists is replaced only once the user confirms.
* [[workspace:open]] `<here|new> <name>` loads it ([[Panel slots and layout]] for the targets): `here` writes the saved variables into the focused workspace — in one go, as [[workspace:send]] does — and puts its panel types back in the slots; `new` does the same in a new workspace on the repository. A variable the save does not hold is left as it is: loading a `layout` keeps the query.
* [[workspace:set-default]] `<name>` makes a saved workspace — of either kind — the repository's default; [[workspace:unset-default]] leaves the repository without one.
* [[workspace:delete-saved]] `<name>` deletes a saved workspace. Deleting the default also unsets it.

There is no listing command: the names are the completions of `workspace:open`.

<<key "workspace:save" ctrl+s>> saves and <<key "workspace:open here" ctrl+o>> opens
here; `i` and `b` before it choose `here` and `new` (<<key "workspace:open new" "b ctrl+o">>),
as for every command with targets.

!! The default workspace

When a workspace takes the repository, the repository's default is loaded into it. A
workspace takes a repository when it is created on it — `workspace:new` — or when the
workspace without one takes the repository picked in the [[repos panel]]
([[Workspaces]]). A fork is a copy and does not load it.

A workspace created by a command or a script — `mf.workspace.create`, a script's
scratch workspace, a [[value picker|Value picker]] — gets the default's ''layout
variables'' only, and only those it does not hold already: its own work comes from
whoever created it, and so do its panels and a picker's seed. When the default is a
`work`, its layout part is what such a workspace gets.

A default that cannot be read — a file edited by hand into something else, one of a
newer format — is reported on the workspace's status bar, and the workspace stays as it
was created.

Changing the default writes one value: which saved workspace it is. Overwriting that
saved workspace changes what the default opens on.

!! On disk

```
.metafolder/gui/workspaces/<name>.json    one saved workspace
.metafolder/gui/default-workspace         the name of the default
```

Written by the GUI, like the input histories ([[Input history]]): ordinary trackable
content, not `internal/`. A saved workspace carries a `version` field, so that a change
of its format can be recognised and converted ([[Persisted format changes]]):

```
{"version": 1, "kind": "work",
 "slots": {"shown": 2, "left": "metarecord-list", "right": "file"},
 "layout": {"metarecord-list:columns": "…", "metarecord-list:column-widths": {…}, …},
 "work": {"metarecord-list:query": "…", "selected_metarecord": {…}, …}}
```

A `layout` save has no `work` member. Reading the layout of either is reading `slots`
and `layout`.

A value a panel no longer understands — a column on a field the repository no longer
has — is handled as a stale variable already is: the panel falls back to its default.
A panel type that no longer exists is put back in its slot all the same, and shows
there that it failed to load, as any panel type that cannot be loaded does
([[Writing a panel type]]).
